behind the scenes.

4
00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.205
But a new customer doesn't
have all that information.

5
00:00:12.538 --> 00:00:16.111
So I want you to imagine looking at
your business through their eyes.

6
00:00:16.787 --> 00:00:19.710
What does someone actually experience
from the moment they discover you?

7
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.523
Let's say you own a detailing business.

8
00:00:22.921 --> 00:00:24.652
Someone wants to get their car detailed.

9
00:00:24.974 --> 00:00:28.530
Maybe they find you on Instagram, Google,
or maybe a friend told them about it.

10
00:00:29.069 --> 00:00:30.665
That's the beginning of their journey.

11
00:00:30.795 --> 00:00:31.795
Discover.

12
00:00:32.469 --> 00:00:35.962
At that moment, they might
know almost nothing about you.

13
00:00:36.554 --> 00:00:39.044
They're just deciding
whether to learn more.

14
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.963
Now think about your own business.

15
00:00:41.442 --> 00:00:44.758
When someone comes across it for
the first time, what do they see?

16
00:00:45.156 --> 00:00:48.016
And is there a clear reason
for them to keep exploring?

17
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.318
Now imagine the person opens your
website or your business profile.

18
00:00:52.696 --> 00:00:54.429
They're trying to
understand what you offer.

19
00:00:54.948 --> 00:00:56.500
What services are available,

20
00:00:56.613 --> 00:00:57.670
what's included.

21
00:00:58.276 --> 00:01:00.159
Does the business serve in their area?

22
00:01:00.562 --> 00:01:03.678
Is it clear what they should
do if they have questions?

23
00:01:04.257 --> 00:01:05.375
That's Understand.

24
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:09.890
But even when someone understands what you
offer, they may still be deciding whether

25
00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:10.930
they trust you.

26
00:01:11.347 --> 00:01:13.256
Maybe they want to see
examples of your work.

27
00:01:13.599 --> 00:01:15.200
Maybe they want to read reviews.

28
00:01:15.393 --> 00:01:17.479
or learn more about the
person behind the business.

29
00:01:17.999 --> 00:01:18.999
That's Trust.

30
00:01:19.449 --> 00:01:22.305
They're trying to decide whether
they feel comfortable moving forward.

31
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.522
Now imagine they're ready.

32
00:01:25.008 --> 00:01:27.478
They want to request a quote
or book an appointment.

33
00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:29.027
That's called Act.

34
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.540
So let me ask you.

35
00:01:30.706 --> 00:01:34.082
How easy is it for someone to take
that first step with your business?

36
00:01:34.547 --> 00:01:35.939
Can they find the right button?

37
00:01:36.174 --> 00:01:38.325
Do they know what information
they have to provide?

38
00:01:38.587 --> 00:01:40.869
Do they understand what
happens after they reach out?

39
00:01:41.407 --> 00:01:45.295
Maybe the process is already clear, or
maybe there's an extra step worth looking

40
00:01:45.335 --> 00:01:46.141
at.

41
00:01:46.181 --> 00:01:47.579
The point is to find out,

42
00:01:47.697 --> 00:01:48.697
not assume.

43
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:52.196
Now here's a part of the journey
I think deserves more attention.

44
00:01:52.602 --> 00:01:54.038
Someone has sent you a message.

45
00:01:54.295 --> 00:01:55.295
What happens next?

46
00:01:55.938 --> 00:01:56.938
That's Response.

47
00:01:57.592 --> 00:01:59.442
Do they know that the
message went through?

48
00:01:59.722 --> 00:02:01.673
Do they know when they might hear back?

49
00:02:02.032 --> 00:02:04.662
And if they need help, is there
a clear way to reach a person?

50
00:02:05.184 --> 00:02:09.654
Technology might help acknowledge that
inquiry. But if the message is automated,

51
00:02:09.694 --> 00:02:13.656
I believe the customers should know
that. The goal is to support a real

52
00:02:13.696 --> 00:02:14.696
conversation,

53
00:02:14.808 --> 00:02:16.729
not pretend automation is a person.

54
00:02:17.733 --> 00:02:20.266
Let's say customers book and
come in for their appointment.

55
00:02:20.508 --> 00:02:23.131
Now they're experiencing
your actual service.

56
00:02:23.482 --> 00:02:24.530
That's Experience.

57
00:02:25.227 --> 00:02:26.774
Were the expectations clear?

58
00:02:26.909 --> 00:02:30.013
Do they know where to go, what
to bring, and what would happen?

59
00:02:30.463 --> 00:02:33.586
And after the service, is it easy
for them to ask another question,

60
00:02:33.703 --> 00:02:35.486
get in touch, or book again?

61
00:02:36.131 --> 00:02:38.224
That's Follow-up and Return.

62
00:02:38.873 --> 00:02:42.275
Your customer's experience doesn't
have to end when the appointment ends.

63
00:02:42.546 --> 00:02:44.130
All these moments are connected.

64
00:02:45.133 --> 00:02:47.871
Now imagine taking that same
journey through your own business.

65
00:02:48.389 --> 00:02:49.389
Discover.

66
00:02:49.438 --> 00:02:50.438
Understand.

67
00:02:50.609 --> 00:02:51.609
Trust.

68
00:02:51.756 --> 00:02:52.641
Act.

69
00:02:52.681 --> 00:02:53.681
Response.

70
00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:54.830
Experience.

71
00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:56.397
Follow-up and Return.

72
00:02:57.205 --> 00:03:00.230
When I talk about diagnosing
a digital customer experience,

73
00:03:00.626 --> 00:03:02.083
that's what I want you to understand.

74
00:03:02.692 --> 00:03:04.485
Not whether your website looks good,

75
00:03:04.769 --> 00:03:07.933
